// Practice quiz: The event loop
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/dom-tooling-and-async/the-event-loop

::o1 promise versus timer::A setTimeout with delay 0 and a resolved promise then are both scheduled. Which callback runs first? {
=The promise callback #Correct. All microtasks drain before the next task.
~The timer callback #Not quite. It waits for a later task, even at 0 ms.
~Whichever was written first #Not quite. The queue decides, not source order.
~They run at the same time #Not quite. One thread runs one thing at a time.
}

::o1 plain code first::Code schedules a promise callback, then logs the word end. Which appears first? {
=end #Correct. Running code finishes before any callback.
~The promise callback output #Not quite. Callbacks wait for the stack to empty.
~Neither, because the promise is pending forever #Not quite. A resolved promise callback does run.
~It depends on network speed #Not quite. No network is involved here.
}

::o1 microtask draining::A microtask schedules another microtask, and a timer callback is also waiting. What runs next after the first microtask? {
=The second microtask #Correct. The queue drains completely before any task.
~The timer callback #Not quite. Tasks wait until microtasks are empty.
~A paint, always #Not quite. Rendering comes after microtasks drain.
~Nothing, until an event fires #Not quite. Queued work continues immediately.
}

::o2 why it freezes::Why does a long synchronous loop freeze the page? {
=It occupies the main thread, so the loop cannot process events, timers or paints #Correct. Nothing can run until it returns.
~The browser limits loops to one per page #Not quite. There is no such limit.
~Loops are always slow in JavaScript #Not quite. Loop speed is not the point; holding the thread is.
~It uses up the task queue #Not quite. The queue is waiting, not used up.
}

::o2 what helps::A heavy calculation runs for several seconds and must keep the page responsive. What fits best? {
=Run it in a Web Worker #Correct. A worker uses another thread, so the main thread stays free.
~Mark the function async #Not quite. That adds no threads and does not yield.
~Wrap it in a promise executor #Not quite. The executor runs synchronously on the main thread.
~Call it inside setTimeout once #Not quite. It still runs in one long task, only later.
}
